From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aaabbk <2268951925@qq.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Nov 2026 19:49:37 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fs: fix close_range() infinite loop when max_fd is ~0U

systemd calls close_range(3, ~0U, 0) to drop inherited file descriptors
before exec'ing each service. In the backported __range_close() the loop
condition is "while (fd <= max_fd)" with both operands unsigned, and fd
is post-incremented inside the body. Once fd walks past UINT_MAX it wraps
to 0, the condition stays true, and the task spins in pick_file() forever
without ever checking for signals.

On a 4.14 kernel this makes systemd freeze during boot: every worker it
spawns (mount, journald, udevadm, tmpfiles, d-sysctl) sits in R state
burning CPU with

    pick_file -> __close_range -> SyS_close_range

and cannot even be killed, because the kernel loop never returns to
userspace. Only a reboot clears it.

Fix: clamp max_fd to the last valid fdtable index before the loop, as
upstream does since v6.0. If last_fd() is absent in your tree, use
fdt->max_fds - 1 under the same lock/RCU protection.

Signed-off-by: aaabbk <2268951925@qq.com>
---
diff --git a/fs/file.c b/fs/file.c
index 4f3e861b4080..c1fdf762bb34 100644
--- a/fs/file.c
+++ b/fs/file.c
@@ -705,6 +705,21 @@ static inline void __range_cloexec(struct files_struct *cur_fds,
 static inline void __range_close(struct files_struct *cur_fds, unsigned int fd,
 				 unsigned int max_fd)
 {
+	unsigned n;
+
+	/*
+	 * Clamp max_fd to the last valid fdtable index.
+	 *
+	 * Callers such as systemd pass max_fd = ~0U (close_range(3, ~0U, 0)).
+	 * Without the clamp, "while (fd <= max_fd)" never terminates once
+	 * "fd++" wraps past UINT_MAX, so the task spins in pick_file()
+	 * forever, burning CPU and hanging systemd (and systemctl).
+	 */
+	spin_lock(&cur_fds->file_lock);
+	n = last_fd(files_fdtable(cur_fds));
+	spin_unlock(&cur_fds->file_lock);
+	max_fd = min(max_fd, n);
+
 	while (fd <= max_fd) {
 		struct file *file;
 
